  "fact_notes": [
    "人物・出来事・制度は架空の教材例。実在の調査・統計・実証結果ではない。制作台帳は来歴確認用。"
  ],
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "id": "p1",
      "sentences": [
        {
          "en": "A fictional student named Mika frequently tells her friends that she can predict which of her plans will succeed.",
          "ja": "架空の生徒ミカは自分の計画のどれが成功するか予測できるとよく友人に話す。"
        },
        {
          "en": "She remembers several occasions on which events developed exactly as she had expected.",
          "ja": "予想通りに出来事が進んだ機会をいくつか覚えている。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Her teacher asks whether she can also recall predictions that were incorrect or too vague to be evaluated afterward.",
          "ja": "教師は誤った予測や後で評価できないほど曖昧な予測も思い出せるか尋ねる。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Mika discovers that describing her overall accuracy is more difficult than recalling a few impressive examples.",
          "ja": "印象的な例を思い出すより全体の正確さを述べる方が難しいと気づく。"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "sentences": [
        {
          "en": "The teacher proposes a simple classroom exercise in which students record predictions before carrying out several ordinary projects.",
          "ja": "教師は普通の活動を実施前に予測を記録する簡単な教室活動を提案する。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Each entry includes a specific expected outcome, the reasons for expecting it, and the degree of confidence assigned to the prediction.",
          "ja": "各記録に具体的な予想結果、その理由、予測への確信度を含める。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Students are encouraged to distinguish what they hope will happen from what they actually consider likely.",
          "ja": "起きてほしいことと実際に起こりそうと考えることを区別するよう促す。"
        },
        {
          "en": "The exercise does not require them to predict events involving personal or sensitive information.",
          "ja": "個人的・機微な情報に関わる出来事の予測は求めない。"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p3",
      "sentences": [
        {
          "en": "After the projects, the students compare their written expectations with the observed results.",
          "ja": "活動後、記録した期待と観察結果を比較する。"
        },
        {
          "en": "They notice that some predictions appeared more precise in their memories than in their original descriptions.",
          "ja": "記憶では元の記述より正確に思えた予測があることに気づく。"
        },
        {
          "en": "A statement that something would probably go well, for instance, leaves considerable room for interpretation once the outcome becomes known.",
          "ja": "例えばうまくいくだろうという発言は結果が既知になるとかなりの解釈の余地を残す。"
        },
        {
          "en": "The notebook helps the students examine what they had actually predicted without silently adjusting their earlier expectations.",
          "ja": "ノートは以前の期待を知らぬうちに変更せず実際の予測を検討する助けとなる。"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p4",
      "sentences": [
        {
          "en": "Some students are disappointed when they discover that their confident predictions were not consistently more accurate than cautious ones.",
          "ja": "自信のある予測が慎重なものより一貫して正確ではないと知り落胆する生徒もいる。"
        },
        {
          "en": "However, the teacher explains that this limited fictional exercise cannot establish the reliability of their judgment in every situation.",
          "ja": "しかし限定的な架空活動では全状況の判断の信頼性を確定できないと説明する。"
        },
        {
          "en": "She encourages them to investigate which assumptions were justified and which depended on information they had not yet obtained.",
          "ja": "どの前提が正当化され、どれが未取得情報に依存したか調べるよう促す。"
        },
        {
          "en": "The purpose is to improve future reasoning rather than reward students for appearing unusually certain about events beyond their control.",
          "ja": "目的は制御外の出来事に異常な確信を示すことを褒めるのでなく今後の推論を改善することだ。"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p5",
      "sentences": [
        {
          "en": "A prediction becomes easier to evaluate when its meaning is established before the relevant outcome is known.",
          "ja": "関連結果が分かる前に意味が定まると予測は評価しやすくなる。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Remembering successful expectations alone may create an incomplete impression of one's ability to anticipate events.",
          "ja": "成功した期待だけ覚えると予測能力の不完全な印象を生み得る。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Recording both accurate and inaccurate judgments can provide a useful starting point for examining how confidence relates to evidence.",
          "ja": "正誤両方の判断記録は確信と根拠の関係を調べる有益な出発点となる。"
        },
        {
          "en": "The notebook therefore represents an effort to learn from uncertainty rather than an attempt to eliminate it.",
          "ja": "ノートは不確実性を排除する試みでなくそこから学ぶ努力を表す。"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
